st give you a few primers on  what devops is for those of you who are  neophytes  in that respect and also to serverless  and fast at a later stage  so first off devops some of you  might know this already so devops is is  really a  is a is a menu is a la carte uh  menu it's a set of practices that  essentially accelerate  the continuous release and re-release a  software components of any kind which  means  including ai software now  typically these two dev and ops  used to be two sides of the same coin  and in principle you would have dev  teams  later decommissioning the work to ops  teams when developer  developers themselves would have  finished  their you know prototypical and  experimentation and validation work on  their own prototypes  and then ops people would put those  prototypes into operations  with the hope that those prototypes  would perform well with the end  customers  but recently a trend has been emerging  in which  a series of practices have been trying  to combine these two silos  uh not only from an organizational  perspective so for example it is  very common nowadays to find what are  known as  devops teams but also from a technical  perspective with all series of practices  to accelerate  the creation the verification the  packaging the releasing  but also the configuration the  integration the deployment  of whatever software components you  might think of  now if you draw this world into  uh into the world of  big data then you have  large-scale data intermixed with devops  software components  so you can think of all sorts of  software components that reflect  big data and data intensive computing uh  just to give you a cue  a couple of examples here you find nosql  databases as opposed to  all sorts of compute platforms like  spark storm flink  kafka whatever hadoop or  whatever the hell you can think of  all of these obviously have specific  challenges but also specific needs and  actors  involved and that's why the trend  of devops being dedicated to specific  big data and data intensive compute  applications has been known or has been  coined as data ops so the  operations the development and  operations around  data intensive computing now again  if you can textualize that even further  specifically designing it for example in  the scope  of predictive proactive preventive  analytics  which harness machine and deep learning  or ai software in general  then you would have you would have  things like  lambda architecture dedicated to  specific machine learning jobs  so a lambda architecture is as  some of you know already a typical  architecture pattern for  big data and data intensive compute  applications but it can be  made to function in the scope of  machine learning operations but from a  more classical standpoint  machine learning operations really  reflect what you are doing day to day  in your data scientific endeavor in  which you have any source of data  sources  uh uh any set of data sources and  a number of libraries and middleware  like pytorch thiano  keras or tensorflow or whatever or any  combination thereof  uh so so this uh has been known to  to to emerge as ml ops  because again you have different  concerns different uh  stakeholders different issues and  challenges  and also technical solutions  which reflect anything between the  continuity of your deployed model  or the automated retraining or active  learning  of your own model as well  now comes serverless computing what is  this serverless computing thing  how does it reflect ml ops where is it  coming from  where is it going well for those of you  who are geeks  and nerds like me you might know  this evolution that i'm showing you here  now  indeed you have things like  originally you had things like  applications being designed for specific  bare metal uh configurations and then  on top of that you had virtualization  you had hypervisoring so you had  uh cloud infrastructures that you could  use to deploy and continuously operate  your applications  but then you came to containers and how  containers changed the world  is that they effectively inserted  onto the technological market the  ability  uh to quickly scale up or down uh  specific applications with their own  technical space into containerized into  packaged formats that would allow them  uh to to to be relocated uh very  very easily this architecture pattern  was known to be  micro services and that's why  this neatly red  shaded box around containers bears the  mark of  micro services there's all sorts of  containerized  micro service trends and technologies  thereof  you can think of i don't know kubernetes  or docker or whatever is your favorite  there's so many out there but at the  same time  containers themselves and microservices  for that matter  did not really specify the level of  granularity  to which your own whatever general  purpose  application was supposed to be designed  and operated  and at which level of abstraction that  decomposition should have been made  that's where  functions come in and where serverless  computing  really realizes its promise because  functions themselves  are essentially fine-grained units of  computation  which are hosted in the cloud and fully  managed by the provider  it doesn't mean that there is no server  serverless by itself  means that you are not  supposed to manage anything from the  server side  the server is a transparent device  to you the functional programmer and  this  also means that you have all sorts of  cost savings  that your workloads are event driven  and also that you are  paying for compute cycles you are  paying for whatever function runtime you  actually realize you are not  paying for the deployment of a specific  work unit or a specific microservice so  even though there is in fact  architecturally a strong synergy with  microservices  there is no restriction from a  deployment perspective your function  is only deployed insofar as  it is consuming computational resources  and this provides for example in the  scope of  amazon web services architecture this  provides for  all sorts of benefits  from a development perspective for  example you would run code without  servers because you can use all sorts of  existing libraries you can make your own  rotating around specific technologies  but also  while you upload your own functions and  configure  the triggers in an event-driven fashion  for those functions  you forget about  managing whatever deployment details and  whatever  serverless specific uh  continuous infrastructure management  considerations you might  you might think of so let me give you an  example of  these sort of these sort of  pipelines or these sort of  functional approaches  imagine the classical extract transform  load data pipeline you have all sorts of  arrivals of raw data  in the data store and you have all sorts  of triggers  being triggered by specific data events  so for example specific types of data  are being input or  specific cues are being filled out  in the scope of queuing management so  you have all sorts of streaming data  coming in  what you as an etl engineer want to do  is  take that data in understand what that  data is about and  process it in such a way that you come  up with  enhanced data now what you see  also in this domain is a  very much transient very much  event driven pipeline which features all  sorts of analysis all sorts of  annotation all sorts of enhancements  being done  uh in uh in the the data pipeline  so how about you actually turn whatever  intelligence  is taking care of the analysis those  annotations those enhancements  and you realize that intelligence with a  series of serverless functions  that are triggered like that  by events in the scope of the  architecture  but also are rigged to consume the least  amount of resources possible what is the  outcome of that well the outcome is  quite obvious  you have improved data integration for  dow